      "title": "[CBC Arts] Forgotten Island a 'love letter' to Filipino mythology, family and home",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://i.cbc.ca/ais/879362ad-8305-4650-bc63-6d135d57807b,1790914634584/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C118%2C1280%2C720%29%3BResize%3D620\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<img alt=\"Animated poster showing two young women and a were-dog.\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://i.cbc.ca/ais/879362ad-8305-4650-bc63-6d135d57807b,1790914634584/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C118%2C1280%2C720%29%3B\" title=\"Forgotten Island is a groundbreaking animated adventure rooted in Filipino culture and mythology, following childhood friends Jo and Raissa as migration, separation and folklore intertwine in a magical world.\" width=\"620\"/><p>Forgotten Island is the first big-budget animated feature to place Filipino culture and mythology at its centre. It follows two childhood friends who are drawn into a fantasy world inspired by Filipino folklore — offering young Filipinos long-awaited representation on screen.</p>",
